#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130085} :activity/id #uuid "b59fa156-cc29-5899-ba12-deabeb536123" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084 :topic/id #uuid "d952f888-5e27-5f27-a3ff-b83b56acd32a" :topic/math-academy-id 2030} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130404 :tutorial/content "Evaluating double integrals by changing the variables to polar coordinates is often easier than using Cartesian coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the disk with center $(0,0)$ and radius $2,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/94/945f3881b5348fffb922d7b57fb42a6262eb03861fb5aa2411b947a16dcb3b59.png)\n\nIf  we express [math]D[/math] as the type I region\n\n\\[\n\\Big\\{(x,y ) \\: : \\:  - 2 \\le x \\le 2, \\: - \\sqrt {4 - {x^2}}  \\le y \\le \\sqrt {4 - {x^2}} \\Big\\}, \n\\]\n\nthen we get\n\n\\[\n\\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}} = \\int_{{\\, - 2}}^{{\\,2}}{{\\int_{{ - \\sqrt {4 - {x^2}} }}^{{\\,\\sqrt {4 - {x^2}} }}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d y}}\\,\\mathrm d x}}.\n\\]\n\nIn its current form, this integral is difficult to evaluate. But there is an easier way!\n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to plane polar coordinates because\n\n- the domain $D$ is a disc of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ which has a simple polar representation, and\n\n- the integrand $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ can also be expressed simply in polar coordinates.\n\nSo, let's transform the entire problem to polar coordinates using the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ defined by\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9901b20d218bef2935dba64612495874d08388dc6d7ae21f8a1de596296bc276.png)\n\nTo express our integral in terms of polar coordinates, we will use the **change of variables formula,** given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, {{\\color{black}{\\mathrm d A}}} = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  {{\\color{black}{r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta}}}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}{\\textrm d A}} = {\\color{black}{r\\,\\textrm d r\\textrm d \\theta}}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of $r.$\n\nNow, applying the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\left( {x^2 + y^2} \\right)\\,\\mathrm d A}} = 8\\pi.\n$$\n\nIn terms of transformations, the change of variables formula maps the non-rectangular integration domain $D$ to the rectangular domain $\\Delta,$ making the integral much easier to evaluate." :tutorial/id #uuid "075c6789-1ae6-51fa-a4f4-f4a2d7ea1459" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4433 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0882db2c-acfd-5afe-af78-e27777d7d1b2" :step/math-academy-id 27266 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129016 :question/id #uuid "d3ae6c85-14bd-57ad-b8cc-1cebb53683b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7346"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354274}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "3cad1b04-2ff0-5a9a-9273-5e82d2a22b0a" :step/math-academy-id 27267 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129017 :question/id #uuid "059deff9-dfa3-5c3e-88b1-194af41d3ed0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7347"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394497}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "d716893c-ef21-57f8-8dc0-b5949c4ea6f9" :step/math-academy-id 27268 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129018 :question/id #uuid "f31dd085-70fa-5eed-adf9-89fb0e6654e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7348"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b97f75d-c784-5f3a-984d-f9a5fdf21d61" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354276}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "8a3064a9-73a4-57bd-9ce0-df4e508faee1" :step/math-academy-id 27269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130406 :tutorial/content "Let's get a little more practice at writing regions in polar coordinates.\n\nConsider the semicircular disc of radius $1$ centered $(1,0)$ in the first quadrant, shown below. The equation of the curve is $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}.$\n\n![](images/0f/0f4aec039a0de31d8f5ea24dc9db8c83f236dfd66e9f181768dae45ab0fd6b07.png)\n\nFirst, we recall that the following equations relate Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the function $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 2\\cos\\theta.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\cos\\theta$ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/35/3584d2f0d9ee2c0ce469bfc117c67c1efd1f4fd08c96c7beef00198624977d06.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/2c/2c5731478941621db03e8949eac217dcdfc604b3efd0312b56c59b9874d6be98.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos\\theta, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04773792-9fe9-5ca8-8434-752f43db5fd7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4454 :tutorial/title "Writing More Complex Regions in Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "d93798a0-43ff-5a1e-a8fe-f7c3040adc4e" :step/math-academy-id 27416 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130219}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130219 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128853 :question/id #uuid "e46cfe46-965d-5c28-90ed-060ec39751a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12376"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1091c5-d81a-565f-a281-b2fb0d00c973" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394552}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Not Centered at the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "181d0ad9-f02a-5dcb-b1f6-c53db9361958" :step/math-academy-id 44390 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130086}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals in Plane Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129371}]}
